>"Ramon Guitart" <ramon.guitart@atosorigin.com> wrote in message
>news:0E16861EE7BCD111BE9400805FE6841F14FF89E0@c1s5x001.cor.srvfarm.origin-it.com...
>> Is it possible to know how many occupied slots do I have without rebooting?

It depends on the system.  Some newer systems log FRU records (with FRU
probably meaning Field Replacable Unit) to the binary error log when they
boot.  Versions of DECevent that shipped on those systems should be able
to interpret the FRU records.  This information may indicate what size
memory boards are installed.

For other systems, there may be a limited number of ways that a specific
amount of memory can be configured.  With suitable system documentation
to know what configuration options were available and the QuickSpecs
or Systems and Options Catalog section for that system, it may be
possible to derive the configuration.

Beyond that I don't believe the kernel keeps that information around
for any command to query.

>> Digital UNIX V4.0D  (Rev. 878); Thu May 25 10:10:54 MET DST 2000
