Rich,

Following up...

The Multia is at a definite disadvantage when it comes to faster CPUs.
The power supply cooling fan is barely adequate.  There is not much
space inside the case to ventilate air, preventing heat buildup.

Rather than the fairly anemic PNY C6 WinChip upgrade, I'll suggest an
Intel 200MHz Pentium MMX OverDrive, if any can be found.  True Intel
CPU with true MMX, fast floating point, and built-in cooling fan.

... Ben Myers  

rjordan@mars.mcs.net (Richard Jordan) wrote:

>This is a followup on my travails upgrading an old Pentium Multia from its
>original 100MHz to the fastest I could go.  Results are mixed, but at least
>it is faster than before.  The system has 96MB RAM, 256K cache, and is
>running the latest/last BIOS 4.05.7.  It will shortly become the toy/
>browser/game box on my network, freeing up an Alphastation 200 for more
>important duty ;).
>
>Tiger Direct has (had) PNY 200MHz upgrades available in liquidation for
>$20 each; this unit works 100% reliably and provides a noticeable visual
>and 'feel' speedup, but actual testing revealed a loss in FP throughput
>compared to the P100.  Memory bandwidth, integer, 2D graphics all showed
>about 50% improvement, 3D, DirectX, and OpenGL showed ~10-20% improvement,
>and disk I/O rates doubled.  The unit is extremely low profile and worked
>consistently with the case closed and the stock fan in operation.  This is
>WinChip-type upgrade, probably a C6.
>
>Powerleap PL-ProMMX-400 will absolutely not operate reliably at 400MHz.
>Even after replacing the low profile cooler (same one as on the PNY)
>with a solid copper big&heavy one it would randomly Dr. Watson in various
>programs.  With the case open, it ran at 366MHz with the new heat sink,
>and would run for a while with the old heat sink but would eventually get
>too warm.  The fastest I could get reliable long term operation in then
>end (with the original heat sink) was at 333MHz, case open, and 300MHz 
>with the case buttoned up.  Clipping the thermistor so the case fan
>ran at full speed did NOT change this.
>
>Note that as shipped the heat sink was attached to the CPU by a 1/2" 
>square of thermal tape.  The chip would get hot enough to hurt when
>running at 400MHz, or 333+ with the case closed.  When I put the copper
>heat sink on I used a full size square of thermal tape covering the entire
>raised surface of the K6-2.  Where the old heat sink rarely got warm to the
>touch unless the case was closed and ambient temps rose, the copper sink
>is defintely pulling more heat away.  I believe that the heat transfer
>through the small section of tape was inadequate, but in the end the new
>heat sink did not allow operation at faster speeds, so who knows.  I had
>hoped to use some kind of clamps and thermal grease for the new heat sink
>but have been unable to get Powerleap to respond to repeated questions
>about the availability of appropriate clamps, so had to resort to the
>tape (which is less efficient in transferring heat than grease/solid
>contact would be).
>
>I am somewhat disappointed by Powerleap's support during this project;
>the support boards were only effective if I went back and kept nudging,
>asking the question repeatedly until someone responded.  Email to the
>support address was also spotty, with my latest queries about the clamps
>still unanswered after almost a week.  I believe that it was an error
>using such a small piece of thermal tape in the first place.
>
>That said, the performance improvement made by the Powerleap was
>substantial, and reliability at the reduced clock rates has been 
>exceptional (in peecee terms).  At 300MHz it is worth about 200%
>improvement in integer and FP over the P100, and more than triple
>the memory bandwidth.  The system is much snappier in use than even
>the PNY upgrade.  When it ran at 400MHz the results were stellar.
>
>So... if you want a decent speedup cheap, look at the PNY.  Outside of 
>the power adapter cord mentioned, none of the other issues listed 
>below are a factor.
>
>If you want
>to go faster, consider a K6-2 upgrade like the Powerleap but be
>aware of the possible problems, such as:
>
>     You need to fabricate your own power adapter cord, unless your
>     Multia came with the power cord for a 3.5" hard drive
>
>     BIOS will report a Pentium at somewher between 75 and 150MHz,
>     not what you actually have.  This may impact some programs
>
>     A low profile cooler may be inadequate to the task of running
>     at the upper speed rates in the hot confines of a closed Multia
>
>     A tall cooler will require case lid surgery.
>
>     If case surgery is performed, air intake through the CPU fan may
>     be enough to feed the exhaust fan, reducing the air intake at the
>     bottom of the case and therefore reducing cooling to the laptop 
>     drive, riser card, and video components (which get HOT).
>
>     Clipping the thermistor for the case fan will help, but makes the
>     system noisy.  The case fan will try to push more air out those
>     tiny holes in the side of the case than will go; increasing the
>     size of the holes (or cutting slots that will line up with those
>     in the plastic lid) helps a lot for airflow and noise.
>
>For now I'm going to keep the Powerleap at 300MHz with the case fan slot
>surgery (but no holes in the lid) and leave it running a few days.
>
