Bob Kaplow wrote:
> had BI and/or XMI i/o bus. The XMI bus may have only been on later 6000
> series machines, like the 6000-600 at my old job.

The XMI bus was used as the system bus on *all* VAX 6000 systems.
At least initially they all shipped with two BI busses as
the I/O busses too.

It was the I/O bus on the VAX 9000 (I think) and on the
VAX 7000 series (which used Laserbus - LSB - as the system bus).

>                                                  There never was a BI SCSI
> controller,

Someone (CMD perhaps?) built one - there was one on ebay the
other week. It may or may not have been re-sold/re-badged
by DEC.
 
>            and at least back then there was no XMI SCSI controller. I think
> there eventually was an XMI SCSI controller for the VAX 7000, but I can't
> recall one ever being backported to the VAX 6000 class machines.

My memory suggests that there was an XMI SCSI controller but
its firmware only worked on DEC 7000 (Alpha) systems and
not on VAX 7000 or VAX 6000 (or VAX 9000) systems.

Again, there may have been one or more 3rd party XMI SCSI
widgets (but I don't recall ever seeing mention of any).

There was an XMI-DSSI widget which worked on both
VAX and Alpha (or at least had variants for each).
If memory serves, that was the KFMSA. With the
appropriate DSSI widget (HSD I think) one could
hang SCSI disks off the DSSI and hence effectively
hang SCSI disks off an XMI bus on a VAX.

