Mark,

Proprietary memory always carries a high price tag.  If the vendor can
even make you THINK the memory is proprietary, you'll pay more for it.
Get the specs of the memory in your DS-20, and match it with good
quality commodity memory.  All the sellers of name-brand Wintel
computers have tried to play this game for years.  Sometimes they
succeed, sometimes not.  Sometimes it's easy to figure out that their
memory game is a crock of manure, because the SIMMs or DIMMs carry the
PNY, Smart, Viking or other OEM brand name and a manufacturer part
number sticker.  Then, too, PNY, Smart, Viking and other major OEMs
play the same game, by giving their DIMMs multiple part numbers, one
per major name brand vendor product line.

I can't claim to have seen all DIMM memory from Alpha systems, but the
memory I have seen appears to be perfectly interchangable with the
ever present Wintel computers.  It's ECC, though, for a server, so it
carries a higher price tag than non-ECC.  The only wrinkle on Alpha
DIMMs is that they are often built for an 83MHz bus, whereas Wintel
PCs use memory for 66, 100, or 133MHz.  So 100MHz ECC DIMMs should
work where 83MHz is called for.  The faster RATED DIMMs simply clock
along at the lower speed.

Now, Compaq/DEC could be even worse.  HP servers require special HP
DIMMs with PALs on them to identify them as HP-branded memory.
Generic memory works in some models, but causes the system startup to
belch out a message saying that the memory is non-HP... Ben Myers

