Right, John.  Intel-made PC100 motherboards, in particular, but others
as well, are too dumb to recognize that PC133 is actually faster than
PC100.  Intel, until recently, had a vested interest in not
recognizing PC133, which is becoming a viable alternative to Rambus,
which Intel backed for a while.  Now, the expense of producing Rambus
memory vs. PC100/PC133 has Intel developing a non-Rambus companion
chipset for the Pentium 4, which is not selling like hot cakes due to
the high cost of Rambus.

Interestingly, you CAN install and run PC133 on a 66MHz FSB Celeron
motherboard, Intel-made or no.

By extension, if a particular Alpha model does a similar Serial
Presence Detect (SPD) check for memory speed vs. the PALs on the
DIMMs, then it will get upset, just like an Intel-made board does.

My, the foregoing is a sentence filled with jargon and acronyms!  More
acronymic than I usually write.

... Ben Myers

