The TX was a dumb frame buffer.  Really.  I used to have a VMS driver and
DDX for it, but I don't think we ever shipped it, I used to run it as a
second head on my system.  It was just too slow to be useable for general
use, I used it to display 24-bit images.  I never figured out why it shipped
on UNIX, except that they supported it on Ultrix, so I suppose it was a
compatability thing.  The only cool thing it had going was the PIP option
that would allow live video to stream into the frame buffer.

And yes, I believe that the only difference between the E2 and E3 was the
amount of offscreen memory for things like Z buffering.

