Xref: supernews.google.com comp.arch:8447 comp.sys.dec:3121


The Alpha 21164 micro-architecture manual says that the TLBs use a
"not most recently used" replacement policy. I can see that it would
be easy to set a tag or pointer to the MRU entry in the fully
associative TLB; you only need to keep track of one thing to do
that. My question is: How does the hardware choose among the other 47
entries to evict one of them? Is there some sort of pseudo-random
scheme?

I take it they are avoiding the pathological behavior of LRU in the
presence of a capacity problem. I am trying to figure out what kind of
code will thrash the "non-MRU" scheme, so details would be
helpful. Thanks to anyone who has knowledge of it. If I have missed
the implementation details in the manuals somewhere, just point me to
the right section.



