Alan Greig <a.greig@virgin.net> writes:

> if anyone wants to watch.
> Includes following sequence:
> "Looks like we had a transient on AC1"
> "We are critical on AC2 centre engine"
> "Critical AC3 right"
> "Lost DCUA centre"
> "DCUB right"
> "Copy that Houston"
> "Go with throttle up"
> "Roger go with throttle up"
> 
> Anyone translate the above?

Yes.

What happened was a short circuit in some wires (due to abrasion - the wide
spread existence of which triggered the grounding of the fleet for repair). 
This lead to one of three (IIRC) redundant power busses and the hardware it
was driving failing. The failed parts were, among others, at least one engine
controller: these are redundant, and the backup took over without a problem. 
Had both controllers failed, the engine would have shut down, and at that
point in the flight, and engine shutdown would have meant a Return to Launch
Site abort, the worst possible case whose survivability is uncertain - doing
U-turns at several Mach isn't easy.

I'm not certain what the abbreviations mean - they should be in the shuttle
reference guide on the KSC web site, but the list of abbreviations doesn't
have them.

They also on that flight had a leak in the cooling pipe that rings the engine
bell on one engine, due to debris. This resulted in somewhat higher
consumption of hydrogen (fuel) that is used as a coolant for the engine bell
before it is burnt, in turn resulting in a slightly lower initial orbit.

The backup control system for the whole system has never been used in flight.

	Jan


