If the disk in the expsnsion box has ULTRIX on it, you can boot it
to single user and use the passwd command to change the password.
It has been a while since I had to do this, but my recollection
is that if you use "boot" and the boot string for the device that
will be a single user boot.  It would be wise to run fsck before
trying to do anything with the file system.

The expansion box may have multiple disks, so you may want to
and boot both.  The console command "cnfg" will give you the
basic high level system configuration, notably the TURBOchannel
number of the SCSI adapter.  I think the Model 200 used slot 5
for the built-in SCSI adapter, but use cnfg to check.  Once
you know where the SCSI adapter(s) is/are, you can see what
is on each one by doing "cnfg #".

To boot from a particular disk:

>>boot tc#/rz#/vmunix

or:

>>boot tc#/rz#/genvmunix

The "tc#" is the TURBOchannel number for the particular adapter
and the # in rz# is the SCSI target-id of the particular disk:

>>boot 5/rz0/vmunix

>>boot 1/rz3/genvmunix

vmunix is the kernel built for the specific configuration.  If
a disk is bootable, odds are it will have a suitable vmunix.
genvmunix is the generic kernel that has all the supported
drivers in it and should be bootable from any disk.
