>in C/C++.

"programmers worth anything" should be involved in the projects they're
coding from the management levels on down.  I remember the migration in
the early '80s to C.  The C proselytes were preaching mass rewrites to
make everything peachy.  One project I was close to at that time was a
project written in Fortran with very heavy real and complex computation
involved.  The proselytes convinced the management that a C rewrite of
the entire project would speed up the end product enormously.  The money
was allocated.  Some of the senior old programmers were retained to help
explain certain areas to the new C proselytes recoding the project.  In
a year and half time, the C rewrite was complete.  Benchmarks showed the
code was more than three times slower.  My guess was all of the overhead
to call library functions to do the real and complex math that was just
a simple in-line in the Fortran.  

Yes, newer compilers would likely have better optimizations to deal with
such issues and, today, the C rewrite might (?) be at par or even faster
than the original.  The problem is bandwagoneering.  All of my neighbors
are installing swimming pools so I have to too mentality.

Anyway, this whole thread is getting silly and stupid.  It's akin to, if
not worse than, the religious OS wars that errupt here from time to time.

