Christof Brass <brass@infopuls.com> writes:

> Secondly, portability is a no issue. If you strive for portability you will 
> have to solve a lot and very different problems than using a/the C/C++ 
> library.

Nonsense. Partablility in all its colours is _the_ issue. Why did IBM invent
S/370? To achieve portability. Why were "higher" programming langauges
invented? To achieve portability. Why were OSes invented? X windows? Tcl/Tk?
Posix? and so on...

I can tell you from personal experince that if you haven't made at least one
application run on about half a dozen platforms, you haven't had a chance to
understand what your programming language (we're talking C, C++, Fortran, Ada,
Pascal, ... here) really means, what is part of the standardized semantics and
what is implementation dependent. Very sobering.

	Jan


