In article <3A9D58D3.92B3B87A@videotron.ca>, JF Mezei <jfmezei.spamnot@videotron.ca> writes:
> Bob Koehler wrote:
>> Really?  Then why is it I've got tons of Java with massive use of
>> dynamically allocated memory structures working quite well in a language
>> which does not have pointers?
> 
> Is Java a compiled language, or a run-time environment ? Doesn't Java execute
> in its own virtual machine with lots of support from the Java engine ? Does
> this qualify as a true compiled language ?

Java is compiled to portable bytecode, and implies a run-time
environment.  The environment depends on context (the environment for an
Applet is more restircted than the environment for an application).

> Also, if there is no concept of pointers in Java, do they allow you to cheat
> and specify pointers to be passed when you call system services etc ?

No.  You have to pass arrays.  Frequently I find myself creating arrays
of 1 element just so I can pass data back through a method argument when
passing only via the return value is insufficient.

None of the built in classes in the Java environments would require a
pointer construct.  

Calls to other environments via the JNI are most easily handled by C/C++
(just because the JNI generates C/C++ code) where conversion between
pointers and arrays is natural.  To a VMS system service, for example,
there's no difference between a C pointer and a C array address since
both pass the address of the data.  IIRC some JNI helper functions use
pointers, but again you may be able to actually use C array syntax.

